UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 29, Lesson 3: AM and PM', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"AM times are from midnight to just before noon; PM times are from noon to just before midnight. Context tells which half of the day.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same method to a larger number or a new context, then compare two solution paths for accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nLabel AM or PM: school starts 9:00; dinner 6:30; bedtime 8:15; sunrise 6:20.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAM; PM; PM; AM (typical).\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking AM/PM only means morning/night without the noon/midnight boundaries confuses midday times.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nSort sixteen events into AM/PM. Write four pairs of the same clock numbers with different AM/PM meanings (e.g. 7:00 AM vs 7:00 PM).\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Mathematics","learning_intention":"AM times are from midnight to just before noon; PM times are from noon to just before midnight. Context tells which half of the day.","lesson_number":3,"lesson_role":"Problem solving","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: place-value chart or number line"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["MA2-NSM-02"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Sort sixteen events into AM/PM. Write four pairs of the same clock numbers with different AM/PM meanings (e.g. 7:00 AM vs 7:00 PM). Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: 7:00 AM is morning breakfast time; 7:00 PM is evening. 12:00 noon is midday; 12:00 midnight starts a new day.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Thinking AM/PM only means morning/night without the noon/midnight boundaries confuses midday times. Correction: AM; PM; PM; AM (typical).\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking AM/PM only means morning/night without the noon/midnight boundaries confuses midday times. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"AM times are from midnight to just before noon; PM times are from noon to just before midnight. Context tells which half of the day. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["AM times are from midnight to just before noon; PM times are from noon to just before midnight. Context tells which half of the day.","Thinking AM/PM only means morning/night without the noon/midnight boundaries confuses midday times.","Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Understand and model\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method.","Copy the model numbers and call that independent work.","Treat a missing zero as if it does not change the value."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Show the working\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific mathematical error, then show the corrected working.","Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Thinking AM/PM only means morning/night without the noon/midnight boundaries confuses midday times.","Treat a missing zero as if it does not change the value.","Skip the working because the first guess looked neat."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Test a misconception\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Use the method on new numbers or a new context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy and units or places.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers.","Skip the working because the first guess looked neat.","Ignore place value and read digits from left to right as ones only."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Transfer to a new problem\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"AM; PM; PM; AM (typical). Useful feedback uses the original numbers and method.","options":["AM; PM; PM; AM (typical).","Thinking AM/PM only means morning/night without the noon/midnight boundaries confuses midday times.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Label AM or PM: school starts 9:00; dinner 6:30; bedtime 8:15; sunrise 6:20.\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer. Useful feedback uses the original numbers and method.","options":["The most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","Writing only the final answer without working does not show the method.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Write the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the met\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"AM; PM; PM; AM (typical). Useful feedback uses the original numbers and method.","options":["AM; PM; PM; AM (typical).","Changing the answer without repairing the method does not fix the error. Thinking AM/PM only means morning/night without the noon/midnight boundaries confuses midday times.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Describe why the warned-against approach gives the wrong value or meaning, then \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers. Useful feedback uses the original numbers and method.","options":["The new problem should use the same method as the model, but contain different numbers.","Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new numbers.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Solve one fresh problem that uses the same skill and write a short reason for yo\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence, not repetition of the worked model, demonstrates the skill.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced relevant working with accurate reasoning and named places or values.","question":"Solve a fresh problem that uses the central skill. Show working and name the places or values that matter.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: 7:00 AM is morning breakfast time; 7:00 PM is evening. 12:00 noon is midday; 12:00 midnight starts a new day.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A correction needs to change value or method, not just make the working longer.","marking_guide":"Require an actual misconception plus corrected working and a method-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely calculation or place-value error, correct it and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Common error: Thinking AM/PM only means morning/night without the noon/midnight boundaries confuses midday times. Correction: AM; PM; PM; AM (typical).","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent working and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-mat-y3-w29-l3","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA mathematics alignment using verified outcome code wording where recorded in lesson-pipeline/sources; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise am.","Read the mission before you start calculating.","Keep the place or unit you are working with in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your working with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the place or distance that carries the method.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore place value or working.","Prefer accurate method over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your working and reason are both present.","Proofread digits, places and operation signs.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact place, value or distance before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key numbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Measurement and Space","success_criteria":["I can use am with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify pm and explain why it matters.","I can practise midnight independently on a new prompt.","I can notice noon needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"AM times are from midnight to just before noon; PM times are from noon to just before midnight. Context tells which half of the day. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["AM times are from midnight to just before noon; PM times are from noon to just before midnight. Context tells which half of the day.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\n7:00 AM is morning breakfast time; 7:00 PM is evening. 12:00 noon is midday; 12:00 midnight starts a new day.\n\nTRY FIRST\nLabel AM or PM: school starts 9:00; dinner 6:30; bedtime 8:15; sunrise 6:20.\n\nFEEDBACK\nAM; PM; PM; AM (typical).","explain":"AM times are from midnight to just before noon; PM times are from noon to just before midnight. Context tells which half of the day.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choo